  "query_preview": "/TOMORROW THROUGH SATURDAY/... \nIssued at 114 PM MDT Sun Jun 21 2026\n\nWeather looks to be quite active this upcoming week as a favorable \npattern for severe storms develops over the region. NW mid level \nmoderate flow will continue through most of the week and several \ndisturbances will move over the region in this flow. Additionally, \nupslope flow will be in place. The combinations of the NW Flow aloft \nand the surface upslope will set up a favorable shear pattern, along \nwith sufficient CAPE, for organized severe storms to develop in the \nafternoon and evening hours. Given the NW flow, storms will have the \npotential to affect the I-25 corridor cities if right turning \nsupercells develop vicinity of the mtn/plains interface. The\nsevere weather threat will likely last into Thursday. By Friday,\nflow aloft finally weakens and the threat for organized\nconvection will decrease. \n\nOver the higher terrain, it will remain hot, dry and breezy at times \nas a modest jet will be over the region. This will keep fire danger \nhigh over the mtns and valleys, A Red Flag warning is in effect \ntomorrow and additional warnings will likely be needed through \nWednesday for the interior mtns and valleys. \n\nLater in the week, guidance indicated that mid level moisture moving \nup from the tropical Pacific will move over S California and then \ninto COlorado, This should bring an increasing chance of precip to \nthe mtns starting Thursday and lasting into Saturday. Likewise, we \nshould hopefully start to see some rain over the higher terrain by \nlater in the week. \n\nIt looks to dry out by Sunday all areas as a rather anonymous mid \nlevel trough develops over the Pacific NW and rather strong flow \n(and very warm temps aloft) move over the region.",
